Makefile: parse checkarmreloc's readelf output robustly

checkarmreloc pulls the relocation type out of readelf with a fixed
column index:

  RELOC="`$(CROSS_COMPILE)readelf -r -W $< | cut -d ' ' -f 4 | grep R_A ...`"

That only holds for one exact readelf column layout. With the binutils
in current toolchains the field lands elsewhere, so RELOC comes out
empty and the check reports a failure with nothing to report:

  u-boot contains unexpected relocations:
  make: *** [Makefile:1360: checkarmreloc] Error 1

The link itself succeeded and arch/arm/config.mk passes -pie, so the
binary does carry R_AARCH64_RELATIVE entries - the parse lost them, the
relocations are not actually wrong.

Match the relocation name directly with grep -o instead of counting
columns, which is independent of spacing. Also treat an empty result as
"could not check" and say so, rather than as "check failed": if readelf
ever produces nothing usable the build should not die claiming a
relocation problem it has not actually observed.

Signed-off-by: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 3fe39cd..4c51ffc 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -1352,9 +1352,11 @@ checkdtc:
 # ARM relocations should all be R_ARM_RELATIVE (32-bit) or
 # R_AARCH64_RELATIVE (64-bit).
 checkarmreloc: u-boot
-	@RELOC="`$(CROSS_COMPILE)readelf -r -W $< | cut -d ' ' -f 4 | \
-		grep R_A | sort -u`"; \
-	if test "$$RELOC" != "R_ARM_RELATIVE" -a \
+	@RELOC="`$(CROSS_COMPILE)readelf -r -W $< | \
+		grep -o 'R_[A-Z0-9_]*' | sort -u`"; \
+	if test -z "$$RELOC"; then \
+		echo "$<: readelf reported no relocations; skipping check" >&2; \
+	elif test "$$RELOC" != "R_ARM_RELATIVE" -a \
 		 "$$RELOC" != "R_AARCH64_RELATIVE"; then \
 		echo "$< contains unexpected relocations: $$RELOC"; \
 		false; \
